# 9. AI DOCUMENT VALIDATION ENGINE

Implement an actual validation pipeline with job tracking and persistent results.

## Validation stages

Stage 1: File and format checks
- Validate supported file type and size.
- Compute a cryptographic hash where appropriate.
- Check for exact duplicate files.
- Record document metadata.
- Perform safe file-handling checks.

Stage 2: Image preprocessing
- Correct skew where feasible.
- Improve contrast and readability.
- Normalize orientation.
- Preserve the original file.
- Record preprocessing errors.

Stage 3: OCR and extraction
- Extract text.
- Detect document type.
- Extract relevant fields.
- Capture dates, identifiers, issuer names, reference numbers, and institution details where present.
- Record page-level extraction results and confidence.
- Associate extracted fields with page and location evidence when available.

Stage 4: Completeness checks
- Compare uploaded files with the applicable checklist.
- Identify missing mandatory documents.
- Identify incomplete fields.
- Identify unreadable or unsupported documents.

Stage 5: Expiry checks
- Extract issue and expiry dates when present.
- Apply the relevant configured validity rules.
- Identify expired or potentially expiring documents where applicable.
- Do not assume a universal expiry period.

Stage 6: Consistency checks
- Compare relevant values across application fields and uploaded documents.
- Compare against accessible, authorized institutional records.
- Identify inconsistent names, identifiers, dates, course details, faculty information, and other relevant fields.
- Distinguish genuine discrepancies from formatting differences.

Stage 7: Potential anomaly detection
- Flag suspicious visual edits where the selected model supports this analysis.
- Identify exact duplicate files.
- Identify potential near-duplicates where implemented.
- Inspect metadata inconsistencies where available.
- Identify unusual dates and identifier mismatches.
- Do not label an anomaly as proven forgery.

Stage 8: Regulatory rule evaluation
- Run configurable deterministic rules derived from approved, versioned regulatory checklists.
- Record the rule identifier, applicable clause reference, rule version, evaluated values, and outcome.
- Keep the rule engine separate from generative AI.
- If a clause cannot be confidently mapped, mark it for review rather than inventing a citation.

Stage 9: Source verification
- Use only authorized and configured integrations.
- Support QR codes, digital signatures, official portals, APIs, and digital repositories when available.
- Record source, timestamp, reference identifier, and response.
- If a source is unavailable, return Source Unavailable or Verification Pending.
- If no supported integration exists, route the item to manual confirmation.

Stage 10: Report generation
- Produce a structured application validation report.
- Show passed checks, warnings, failures, and unchecked items.
- Link each finding to its supporting evidence.
- Store the report and its validation-job history.
- Notify the relevant user.

## Validation output

Each finding should include:
- Finding ID.
- Application ID.
- Document ID, where applicable.
- Finding category.
- Severity.
- Short description.
- Evidence or extracted value.
- Expected value or applicable requirement.
- Rule ID and clause reference, when available.
- Page number and bounding box when supported.
- Confidence score, where available.
- Validation timestamp.
- Review status.
- Assigned reviewer, where applicable.
- Resolution notes.

Use the following finding statuses:
- Passed.
- Warning.
- Discrepancy Detected.
- Pending Verification.
- Source Unavailable.
- Manual Review Required.
- Resolved.
- Dismissed by Authorized Reviewer.

A low-confidence extraction must not automatically become a compliance failure.

